%erg v1
Title: Erg struct: schema-literal fields, drop accessors and the headers map
Created: 2026-05-11
Author: claude-sonnet-4-6

--- log ---
2026-05-11T00:00Z claude-sonnet-4-6 created

--- body ---
## Context
This is the unfinished half of ticket 0105. PR #115 added accessor methods
and unexported the headers field, but the struct itself still stores values
in a `map[string][]string` bag and the accessors do runtime string-keyed
lookups. The struct does not mirror the spec's header table; rule checks
on a typo like `"Blocked-By"` would silently miss values.

The spec defines a closed set of 6 headers; the struct should reflect it
directly with typed fields, and the trivial accessor methods that exist
only as map wrappers go away.

## Current shape (verbatim, erg.go:121-135)
```go
type Erg struct {
    Path     string
    headers  map[string][]string
    LogLines []string
    Body     string
    HasMagic bool
    LogSepCount  int
    BodySepCount int
    ClosedInLog  bool
    ClosedInBody bool
}
```

## Proposed shape
```go
type Erg struct {
    Path     string
    HasMagic bool

    // v1 headers — typed fields populated from first occurrence
    Title      string   // required, non-empty (spec tightened — see below)
    Created    string   // required, non-empty
    Author     string   // required, non-empty
    Closed     string   // optional; non-empty when present
    BlockedBys []string // possibly empty; one entry per `Blocked-by:` line
    Tags       []string // possibly empty; one entry per `Tag:` line (after 0106)

    LogLines   []string

    Body       string

    // Diagnostic trace — TEMPORARY; collapses in the eventual parser/
    // validator merge (a follow-up refactor), at which point parser emits
    // errors directly and these fields disappear.
    Unknown            []string  // unknown header keys seen
    RepeatedSingletons []string  // singleton keys seen more than once
    ClosedEmpty        bool      // `Closed:` line appeared with empty value
    ClosedInLog        bool
    ClosedInBody       bool
    HasLogSep          bool      // first `--- log ---` was seen
    HasBodySep         bool      // first `--- body ---` was seen
}
```

Note plural field names `BlockedBys` and `Tags`: the field is a list of
lines (possibly empty). Header name in the file remains singular
(`Blocked-by:`, `Tag:` after 0106) per the repeatable-header convention.

No `ClosedSet` flag. Empty `Closed:` lines are recorded via `ClosedEmpty`
which the validator (and later, the merged parse-validate pass) consumes
as an error signal — no need for a tri-state Closed value.

## Spec tightening (required headers)
Update `tickets/spec-erg-v1.md`: required headers (`Title`, `Created`,
`Author`) must have a non-empty value. Today the validator passes
`Title: ` (key present, value empty) because rule 2 only checks key
presence. After this refactor, empty required headers fail validation.

## Spec relaxation (separator duplicates)
Validator rule 12 today requires each separator (`--- log ---`,
`--- body ---`) to appear **exactly once**. This is overstrict: legitimate
bodies will contain those literals — heredoc examples, format
documentation, this very ticket's body that quotes the strings. The
parser already uses first-occurrence semantics for section transitions
(erg.go:357-369), but the validator rejects the file based on the count.

Change:
- Drop the "appears N times" branch of rule 12; keep only "missing".
- Spec rewording: "The first `--- log ---` and the first `--- body ---`
  (in order) are the section separators. Subsequent occurrences inside
  body are body text."
- Struct change: replace `LogSepCount`/`BodySepCount` (which existed only
  to drive the now-deleted overstrict check) with `HasLogSep`/`HasBodySep`
  bools in the diagnostic trace.

## Accessor methods go away
All trivial accessors are deleted; call sites read the field directly.
The two methods with real logic stay (they encapsulate non-trivial
behaviour, not map indirection):
- `Closed()` — path-based test OR non-empty header value
- `BlockedByRefs()` — parses every raw ref string

| Today               | After                                          |
|---------------------|------------------------------------------------|
| `t.Title()`         | `t.Title` (direct read)                        |
| `t.Created()`       | `t.Created`                                    |
| `t.Author()`        | `t.Author`                                     |
| `t.ClosedHeader()`  | `t.Closed != ""`                               |
| `t.BlockedBy()`     | `t.BlockedBys`                                 |
| `t.Tags()`          | `t.Tags`                                       |
| `t.Closed()`        | unchanged method body, reads `t.Closed` field  |
| `t.BlockedByRefs()` | unchanged method, iterates `t.BlockedBys`      |

## Call-site impact
21 accessor call sites across 6 files. After this refactor:
- archive.go: `Closed()`, `BlockedByRefs()`, `ClosedHeader()` → keep first two; the third becomes `t.Closed != ""`
- check.go:   `ClosedHeader()`, `Closed()` ×2, `BlockedByRefs()` → similar
- close.go:   `Closed()` ×2, `BlockedBy()` → `t.BlockedBys`
- ready.go:   `Closed()` ×2, `Tags()`, `BlockedByRefs()`, `Title()` → field reads where trivial
- validate.go: `Created()` → `t.Created`; `BlockedByRefs()` ×2 stays; the 4 direct `t.headers[...]` accesses switch to typed fields + trace

## Test impact
- erg_test.go: parser tests construct `Erg` literals — update to new shape.
- validate_test.go: rule 2 fixtures may need adjustment for the
  empty-required-header tightening; add a positive test for it.
- Compile-time safety test: a misspelled-field access fails to compile
  (the file simply doesn't build). Add this as a documented assertion
  in a comment near the struct, since there is nothing to "run."

## Out of scope (held back from 0105's scope creep)
- **Parser/validator merge into one pass.** The diagnostic-trace fields
  (`Unknown`, `RepeatedSingletons`, `ClosedEmpty`, `ClosedInLog`,
  `ClosedInBody`, `HasLogSep`, `HasBodySep`) are placeholders that
  disappear in that follow-up. File as a separate ticket when ready.
- **Type tightening of header fields**: `BlockedBys []string` → `[]Ref`,
  `Created string` → `time.Time`. These eliminate the surviving
  `BlockedByRefs()` method and push date parsing into the parser. File
  as a separate ticket after the merge ticket lands (the merge is the
  natural place to introduce parse-time type errors).
- Renaming `Tags` → `Tag` (header rename) is ticket 0106. If 0106 lands
  first, this ticket reads `Tag:` headers into the `Tags` field.
- Function-level splits in `parseErg`; only struct shape and the
  accessor surface change.

## Endpoint shape (post-merge, post-type-tightening)
This ticket plus the two follow-ups land at:

```go
type Erg struct {
    Path     string
    HasMagic bool

    Title      string    // required, non-empty
    Created    time.Time // required, parsed at parse time
    Author     string    // required, non-empty
    Closed     string    // optional; non-empty when present
    BlockedBys []Ref     // possibly empty; each ref parsed at parse time
    Tags       []string  // possibly empty

    LogLines []string
    Body     string
}

func parseErg(path string) (Erg, []ParseError)
```

Zero accessor methods (`Closed()` survives only because path-OR-header is
intrinsic — and is a free function or method as taste dictates). The
struct is a literal projection of the spec.

## Dependencies
- Soft dep: ticket 0115 (split Ref into ref.go). If 0115 lands first,
  this ticket touches only the Erg portion of erg.go. Order is cheap to
  swap either way.

## Exit criteria
- `Erg` struct fields correspond 1-to-1 to the v1 header table.
- All trivial accessor methods are removed; only `Closed()` and
  `BlockedByRefs()` remain.
- Spec updated: required headers must be non-empty.
- Spec updated: separator-duplicates rule relaxed to first-occurrence
  semantics; validator rule 12 trimmed accordingly.
- `make test` passes; new tests assert (a) empty required header is
  rejected, and (b) a body containing `--- log ---`/`--- body ---` as
  text is accepted.
